WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.500
It sounds like an urban legend, but&nbsp;
actor Jim Caviezel was legitimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.346
struck by lightning on the set of&nbsp;
"The Passion of the Christ." 

00:00:07.346 --> 00:00:10.668
And&nbsp;that's just one of the setbacks&nbsp;
the actor faced during filming.

00:00:10.668 --> 00:00:14.520
Jim Caviezel is lighting up Hollywood&nbsp;
with his leading role as Tim Ballard in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.120
"Sound of Freedom." Fans of the movie&nbsp;
who haven't kept up with the actor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.900
career may not remember that Caviezel got&nbsp;
lit up himself while shooting a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.150
movie nearly twenty years ago, and&nbsp;
luckily survived to tell the tale.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:32.000
In director Mel Gibson's controversial but highly&nbsp;
successful 2004 film "The Passion of the Christ,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.080
Caviezel took on arguably his biggest role as&nbsp;
Jesus. During filming, the actor's experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.223
was fraught with peril, including quite&nbsp;
literally being struck by lightning.

00:00:41.223 --> 00:00:46.620
In 2003, well into the production of "The Passion&nbsp;
of the Christ," Entertainment Weekly reported on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.580
the lightning strike that occurred while filming&nbsp;
a scene. Caviezel wasn't the only one hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:56.040
either. Assistant director Jan Michelini was also&nbsp;
struck by the bolt, marking the second time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.700
he had been hit by lightning while working on the&nbsp;
movie. Producer Steve McEveety told the outlet,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.638
"I'm about a hundred feet away from them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.638 --> 00:01:08.040
when I glance over and see lightning coming out&nbsp;
of Caviezel's ears. Both Caviezel and Michelini&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.840
got struck. The main bolt hit Caviezel and&nbsp;
one of its forks hit Michelini's umbrella."

00:01:13.055 --> 00:01:16.235
"When I was hit, everybody fell on their face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.235 --> 00:01:19.200
The ground shook. That was&nbsp;
the last shot of the movie."

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.820
Both Caviezel and Michelini survived&nbsp;
the lightning strike and later reports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.560
claimed they were unharmed by the incident. But&nbsp;
that's not the complete truth. In an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.600
with Peggy Noonan of The Words Group, Caviezel&nbsp;
described what the experience felt like. He said,

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:37.560
"About four seconds before it happened it&nbsp;
was quiet, and then it was like someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.880
slapped my ears. I had seven or eight&nbsp;
seconds of, like, a pink, fuzzy color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.440
and people started screaming. They said I&nbsp;
had fire on the left side of my head and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:51.226
light around my body. All I can tell you is that&nbsp;
I looked like I went to Don King's hairstylist."

00:01:51.226 --> 00:01:55.740
Between the lightning and numerous other injuries&nbsp;
sustained during the production, the actor ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.720
up in the operating room twice for serious&nbsp;
medical procedures. He told Catholic Courier,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.060
"It nearly killed me. Not many people get struck&nbsp;
by lightning; I did. Five and a half months of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:11.461
cold. I had to have two heart surgeries, including&nbsp;
open-heart surgery, because of that film."

00:02:11.461 --> 00:02:15.480
The lightning strike is just one item on the&nbsp;
laundry list of injuries Caviezel ended up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.980
throughout the production of "The Passion of the&nbsp;
Christ." In one instance, he sustained a deep gash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.820
in his back as the result of an accident&nbsp;
in a scene where Jesus is being whipped.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:27.240
Caviezel told "Today" that another&nbsp;
actor missed the board protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.412
him and accidentally struck his back&nbsp;
with their whip. Caviezel recalled,

00:02:31.412 --> 00:02:34.920
"I turned around and looked at the guy,&nbsp;
and I tell you, I may be playing Jesus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.471
but I felt like Satan at that moment. I turned to&nbsp;
him, a couple of expletives came out of my mouth."

00:02:40.643 --> 00:02:42.863
He would later get struck in the back again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.863 --> 00:02:46.920
which left him with a 14-inch gash that&nbsp;
would eventually turn into a lasting scar.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.360
Caviezel also dislocated his shoulder&nbsp;
while carrying a large cross and caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.320
pneumonia because of filming in frigid&nbsp;
temperatures for extended periods of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.440
time. Despite all of the ways the&nbsp;
movie damaged the actor's body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:03.360
Caviezel maintains that he doesn't regret&nbsp;
working on the project. He told Catholic Courier,

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.240
"If we had shot that film in a&nbsp;
studio, you wouldn't have seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.334
that performance. Was it worth it? Absolutely."

